        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Velvet Revolution</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> (Národní Street, Wenceslas Square, Letná) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Bratislava</a>, with strikes and demonstrations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrava">Ostrava</a> and all the major cities</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">dissent under one-party rule, 1948–1989, with the first anti-government demonstrations in 1988; the revolution, 17–28 November 1989, with demonstrations into late December; the first largely non-communist government, 10 December 1989; Havel elected president, 29 December 1989; the first democratic elections, June 1990; the peaceful split of the country, 31 December 1992</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Leading figures</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaclav-havel">Václav Havel</a>, playwright and human rights activist, founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civic-forum">Civic Forum</a> and president from 29 December 1989; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-dubcek">Alexander Dubček</a>, leader of the Prague Spring, speaker of the federal parliament; Milan Kňažko and Ján Budaj, founders of Public Against Violence; Prime Minister Ladislav Adamec; the Communist leader Miloš Jakeš; President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-husak">Gustáv Husák</a>; Rita Klímová, who coined the name</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">the end of the Communist Party’s monopoly of power, a largely non-communist government and Havel’s presidency; the withdrawal of about 73,500 Soviet troops; free elections in June 1990; a parliamentary republic and a market economy; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> split peacefully into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a> and the Slovak Republic at the end of 1992</span></div>
