    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of the Velvet Revolution</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-dubcek.jpg" alt="Alexander Dubček" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Alexander Dubček <span class="kid-tale-cr">CTK, CC0</span></figcaption></figure><p>From 1948, Czechoslovakia was ruled by the Communist Party, and no other party could work openly.</p><p>People were punished for having supported Alexander Dubček, leader of the Prague Spring of 1968.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/velvet-revolution.jpg" alt="Velvet Revolution" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Velvet Revolution <span class="kid-tale-cr">ŠJů (cs:ŠJů), CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>In November 1989, police attacked a student march in Prague.</p><p>Students and theatres went on strike, and huge crowds filled Wenceslas Square.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/civic-forum.jpg" alt="Civic Forum" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Civic Forum <span class="kid-tale-cr">Pavel Šťastný, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>Václav Havel, a writer of plays, founded a group called Civic Forum.</p><p>Crowds jingled their keys, a sign meaning “unlock the doors” and “goodbye” to the Communists.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gustav-husak.jpg" alt="Gustáv Husák" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Gustáv Husák <span class="kid-tale-cr">kalendarakcii.bratislava.sk, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>After a two-hour strike by workers across the country, the Communist Party gave up its power.</p><p>President Gustáv Husák swore in a government not run by the Communists, and then resigned.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vaclav-havel.jpg" alt="Václav Havel" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Václav Havel <span class="kid-tale-cr">Kingkongphoto &amp; www.celebrity-photos.com from Laurel Maryland, USA, CC BY-SA 2.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Havel was elected president, and in 1990 the country held free elections.</p><p>In 1992 Czechoslovakia split peacefully in two: the Czech Republic and the Slovak Republic.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A police attack on a student march in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> on 17 November 1989, the 50th anniversary of the Nazi storming of Prague’s universities, set off the revolution.</li><li>Within eleven days mass demonstrations, strikes by students and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a> and a two-hour general strike forced the Communist Party to give up its monopoly of power.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaclav-havel">Václav Havel</a>, a dissident playwright, was elected president on 29 December 1989, and in June 1990 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> held its first democratic elections since 1946.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Demonstrators jingled their keys, a sign that meant both the unlocking of doors and “Goodbye, it’s time to go home” to the Communists; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>’s 2-euro coin for the 20th anniversary in 2009 shows a bell with a key beside its clapper.</p></aside>
    </section>

